Wizards start out very vulnerable so in the early game playing a wizard
is a matter of casting force bolt while running away plus managing your
pet cat.

> How *does* one play a wizard, anyhow?  They can't do
> melee in the early game without dying of hitpoint loss, can't
> cast spells to any significant extent before Sokoban without
> dying of hunger, and don't start with any projectiles, so you
> pretty much have to make your pet kill everything, and kittens
> don't seem to be as good for that as dogs, in my experience.
> There must be some crucial strategic approach I'm missing.

Weapons - The starting quarterstaff is a trap to get you to waste skill
slots.  Use it if you need to and enhance it if you must to survive but
learn defense early.  Gather stacks of daggers in the mines and
gradually evolve them to a large stack of highly enchanted elven daggers
by the middle game.  Enhance dagger early and often.  First elven dagger
that does not stack, figure it's enchanted so make it Sting.  Only use
it for melee in an emergency.  Go for Magicbane at the first altar and
use it as your primary defense for the rest of the game.  If you can get
Stormbringer it's a wonderful melee alternate weapon for the midgame
when you start encountering popcorn monsters or get occasionally trapped
into melee.

Armor - In the mines you'll get mithril from a dwarf or hobbit.  Wear it
and play as a dagger thrower.  Find a studded leather armor if you can
and when you build confidence that you can survive by running and
firing, switch to it.  Then you'll be able to run, cast and fire.  So it
goes cast very early, fire once in the mines, cast and fire say below
Sokoban.  Get the amulet in Sokoban and combined with your initial cloak
you've got the important reflection and magic resistances covered.  Get
the bag in Sokoban and you'll want to wish for SDSM.  You only become a
tank after you've gotten through both castles.

Casting - Once your intelligence gives you hunger free casting the
create monster spell ensures you'll never starve.  Plus us it at any
altar to get unlimited books and protection.  A wizard might never need
a robe.


